        "code": {
          "text": "Superficial metallic foreign body injury of the left cornea with healing corneal abrasion; non-penetrating and improving"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-1",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-1",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Reported left eye symptoms and associated symptoms"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "At initial injury, sudden left eye irritation and watering after suspected metal entered the eye while grinding. Today, mild scratchy sensation and intermittent awareness of the left eye, especially at the end of the workday. No significant pain, photophobia, purulent discharge, flashes, floaters, curtain or significant blur. Vision reported back to normal. No fever or systemic symptoms."
      }
    },
    {
      "fullUrl": "https://example.org/fhir/Observation/observation-2",
      "resource": {
        "resourceType": "Observation",
        "id": "observation-2",
        "status": "final",
        "code": {
          "text": "Emergency department and ophthalmology findings on day of injury"
        },
        "subject": {
          "reference": "Patient/patient-1"
        },
        "valueString": "Small superficial metallic foreign body removed from the left cornea, with a small residual epithelial defect. Seidel negative; no penetrating injury or open globe. Anterior chamber deep and quiet; pupil round and reactive; no hypopyon. No intraocular foreign body on emergency department ocular ultrasound. Visual acuity: right 6/6, left 6/9 improving to 6/6 with pinhole. Ophthalmology documented low suspicion of penetrating injury; CT/X-ray not indicated. No endophthalmitis at emergency department review."
      }
    },
